1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:03,977
Rub a balloon on your hair and press it to a wall.

2
00:00:04,027 --> 00:00:05,057
It stays.

3
00:00:05,147 --> 00:00:06,617
Nothing is holding it.

4
00:00:06,747 --> 00:00:10,927
No glue, no string, no contact at all between the charges

5
00:00:10,947 --> 00:00:12,017
doing the work.

6
00:00:12,187 --> 00:00:15,977
Something is reaching across empty space and pulling.

7
00:00:16,107 --> 00:00:18,647
That something is the electric force,

8
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:21,137
and it is astonishingly strong.

9
00:00:21,307 --> 00:00:24,487
Compare it with gravity for a single electron and a single

10
00:00:24,507 --> 00:00:29,057
proton: the electric pull is stronger by a thousand billion

11
00:00:29,147 --> 00:00:31,777
billion billion billion times.

12
00:00:31,947 --> 00:00:34,257
So why don't we feel it everywhere?

13
00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:38,247
Because matter is balanced, almost perfectly.

14
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:40,847
If you and a friend stood at arm's length,

15
00:00:40,867 --> 00:00:44,167
and each of you had just one percent more electrons than

16
00:00:44,187 --> 00:00:47,487
protons, the push between you would be enough to lift

17
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:50,647
a weight equal to the entire Earth.

18
00:00:50,667 --> 00:00:54,247
Every wire, every motor and every circuit you will ever

19
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:56,657
study runs on this force.

20
00:00:56,747 --> 00:00:58,657
So let's see where it comes from.

21
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:03,223
People noticed this force a very long time ago.

22
00:01:03,313 --> 00:01:07,453
Around six hundred BC, the Greek thinker Thales recorded

23
00:01:07,473 --> 00:01:11,133
that amber, rubbed with wool, would pull in feathers and

24
00:01:11,153 --> 00:01:12,183
bits of straw.

25
00:01:12,593 --> 00:01:15,303
The Greek word for amber is elektron.

26
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:18,583
Every word we use today, electricity,

27
00:01:18,793 --> 00:01:22,253
electron, electronics, comes from a piece of fossil tree

28
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:23,063
resin.

29
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:26,183
For two thousand years it stayed a curiosity.

30
00:01:26,593 --> 00:01:28,813
Then in the year sixteen hundred,

31
00:01:28,833 --> 00:01:32,423
William Gilbert, physician to Queen Elizabeth the First,

32
00:01:32,513 --> 00:01:35,973
tested hundreds of materials and found that glass,

33
00:01:35,993 --> 00:01:39,463
sulphur, wax and many gems did the same thing.

34
00:01:39,713 --> 00:01:42,583
He gave the effect its name: electric.

35
00:01:42,993 --> 00:01:46,253
In seventeen thirty-three, the Frenchman Charles du Fay

36
00:01:46,273 --> 00:01:47,783
found something stranger.

37
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:50,103
There were two kinds of electricity.

38
00:01:50,193 --> 00:01:53,693
Objects charged the same way pushed each other apart,

39
00:01:53,713 --> 00:01:57,103
and objects charged in different ways pulled together.

40
00:01:57,233 --> 00:02:00,413
A few years later, Benjamin Franklin proposed that there

41
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:02,773
was really one electrical substance,

42
00:02:02,793 --> 00:02:06,143
and that an object could have too much of it or too little.

43
00:02:06,193 --> 00:02:09,463
He called the two conditions positive and negative.

44
00:02:09,713 --> 00:02:13,623
We still use his names, and his choice of which was which.

45
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,510
The source is a property of matter called charge.

46
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,990
It is as basic as mass.

47
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,910
Mass tells you how strongly something responds to gravity.

48
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,500
Charge tells you how strongly it responds to the electric

49
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:29,390
force.

50
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:33,670
But charge comes in two kinds, and that changes everything.

51
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:36,430
We call them positive and negative.

52
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:39,140
Protons carry the positive kind,

53
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:43,140
electrons the negative kind, and the two amounts are exactly

54
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:44,590
equal in size.

55
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:48,150
Two charges of the same kind push each other apart.

56
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:51,030
Two of opposite kinds pull together.

57
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:55,060
An ordinary atom has as many electrons as protons,

58
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,550
so from outside it looks neutral.

59
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:01,060
Charging an object does not create anything.

60
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:04,740
It only moves electrons from one place to another.

61
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:08,020
When you rub the balloon, electrons leave your hair and

62
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:09,380
settle on the rubber.

63
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,980
The balloon ends up negative, your hair ends up positive

64
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,740
by the same amount, and the total has not changed.

65
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,740
Charge is never made and never destroyed.

66
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:21,940
It is only moved.

67
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:25,060
Franklin had to guess which way his electrical substance

68
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:27,620
moved, and he guessed wrong.

69
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:30,420
The thing that actually moves is the electron,

70
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:31,380
discovered by J.

71
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:31,580
J.

72
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,860
Thomson in eighteen ninety-seven,

73
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:36,750
and by Franklin's labels it is negative.

74
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:40,420
That is why, to this day, the current we draw in a circuit

75
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:44,030
points the opposite way to the electrons that carry it.

76
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:46,790
Charge also comes in whole steps.

77
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:49,260
You can have one electron's worth of charge,

78
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:52,750
or two, or a billion, but never half.

79
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,780
Robert Millikan showed this around nineteen ten,

80
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,140
by balancing tiny charged oil drops between two metal

81
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:03,060
plates and finding that their charge always came in multiples

82
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,300
of one smallest amount.

83
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,540
We measure charge in coulombs.

84
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:10,980
One coulomb is the charge of about six billion billion

85
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,260
electrons, and it is an enormous amount.

86
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:17,710
A whole lightning bolt moves only about fifteen.

87
00:04:19,067 --> 00:04:22,057
How strong is the force between two charges?

88
00:04:22,187 --> 00:04:25,527
In seventeen eighty-five, the French engineer Charles

89
00:04:25,547 --> 00:04:28,087
Coulomb found a way to measure it.

90
00:04:28,107 --> 00:04:31,207
He hung a light rod from a very fine wire,

91
00:04:31,227 --> 00:04:34,087
with a small charged ball on one end.

92
00:04:34,107 --> 00:04:36,807
When he brought a second charged ball close,

93
00:04:36,827 --> 00:04:40,337
the rod turned, and the twist in the wire told him the

94
00:04:40,387 --> 00:04:41,207
force.

95
00:04:41,227 --> 00:04:44,727
The forces were tiny, and his balance was sensitive enough

96
00:04:44,747 --> 00:04:45,927
to weigh them.

97
00:04:45,947 --> 00:04:48,417
He found two simple rules.

98
00:04:48,467 --> 00:04:51,767
First, the force grows with each charge.

99
00:04:51,787 --> 00:04:54,487
Double one charge and the force doubles.

100
00:04:54,507 --> 00:04:58,017
Double both and it is four times as strong.

101
00:04:58,107 --> 00:05:00,967
Second, the force falls with distance,

102
00:05:00,987 --> 00:05:02,847
and it falls fast.

103
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:06,167
Move the charges twice as far apart and the force drops

104
00:05:06,187 --> 00:05:07,407
to a quarter.

105
00:05:07,427 --> 00:05:09,927
Three times as far, a ninth.

106
00:05:09,947 --> 00:05:12,287
This is an inverse square law.

107
00:05:12,307 --> 00:05:14,487
Here is why it has that shape.

108
00:05:14,507 --> 00:05:18,007
Picture the influence of a charge spreading outward evenly

109
00:05:18,027 --> 00:05:19,634
in every direction.

110
00:05:19,654 --> 00:05:22,860
At a distance r, it is spread over a sphere,

111
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:26,087
and the area of a sphere grows with r squared.

112
00:05:26,107 --> 00:05:29,527
The same influence over four times the area is a quarter

113
00:05:29,547 --> 00:05:30,647
as strong.

114
00:05:30,667 --> 00:05:34,087
Put the two rules together and you have Coulomb's law.

115
00:05:34,107 --> 00:05:37,767
The force equals a constant, times the first charge,

116
00:05:37,787 --> 00:05:41,247
times the second, divided by the distance squared.

117
00:05:41,267 --> 00:05:44,887
That constant is huge: nine billion.

118
00:05:44,907 --> 00:05:47,074
Two charges of one coulomb each,

119
00:05:47,094 --> 00:05:50,087
a metre apart, would push on each other with the weight

120
00:05:50,107 --> 00:05:52,487
of nearly a million tonnes.

121
00:05:52,507 --> 00:05:55,207
That is why you never meet a loose coulomb.

122
00:05:55,227 --> 00:05:57,420
And when there are more than two charges,

123
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:58,767
nothing new is needed.

124
00:05:58,787 --> 00:06:01,367
Work out the force from each one separately,

125
00:06:01,387 --> 00:06:03,137
then add them as arrows.

126
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:07,690
Coulomb's law tells us how big the force is.

127
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:11,040
It does not tell us how one charge can push on another

128
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:12,170
without touching it.

129
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:15,650
How does the second charge know the first one is there?

130
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:20,010
The answer is one of the most powerful ideas in all of

131
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:20,890
physics.

132
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:24,690
A charge does not act on other charges directly.

133
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,690
It changes the space around itself.

134
00:06:27,860 --> 00:06:32,000
Every point in that space now carries a kind of instruction:

135
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:35,040
if a charge were placed here, it would be pushed this

136
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,120
hard, in this direction.

137
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:40,960
That condition of space is called the electric field.

138
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:43,120
The idea came from Michael Faraday,

139
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:45,520
in the eighteen-thirties and forties.

140
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:49,040
Faraday was a blacksmith's son who left school at thirteen

141
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:52,240
to work for a bookbinder, and he never learned advanced

142
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:53,560
mathematics.

143
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,880
So he thought in pictures.

144
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:59,040
Where others saw empty space between two charges,

145
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:02,050
he saw it filled with lines of force.

146
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:05,840
Twenty years later, James Clerk Maxwell turned Faraday's

147
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:09,760
pictures into equations, and those equations still run

148
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:11,970
the whole of electrical engineering.

149
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:15,600
To measure it, place a small positive test charge at a

150
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:18,130
point and see what force it feels.

151
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:21,530
Then divide that force by the size of the test charge.

152
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:24,240
What is left belongs to the place,

153
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:25,370
not to the tester.

154
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:28,080
It is the force per unit of charge,

155
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:30,290
measured in newtons per coulomb.

156
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:31,610
We call it E.

157
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:35,170
Here is what textbooks rarely say plainly.

158
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:38,600
The field is there whether or not anything is around to

159
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:39,640
feel it.

160
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:42,440
Take the test charge away, and the field remains,

161
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:44,960
at every point, waiting.

162
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:47,800
Think of a weather map covered in wind arrows.

163
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:50,520
The wind is there at every spot on the map,

164
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:53,200
whether or not a kite happens to be flying.

165
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:56,720
A charge placed in a field simply feels the arrow at the

166
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:58,240
place where it sits.

167
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:01,600
The force on it is its charge times the field:

168
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:03,770
F equals q E.

169
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:07,040
For a single charge, the field points straight away from

170
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:10,320
it if the charge is positive, and straight in toward it

171
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,560
if negative.

172
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:14,160
And it weakens with the square of the distance,

173
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:16,250
exactly as Coulomb found.

174
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:21,860
Arrows at every point quickly become a crowded picture.

175
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:24,940
So we draw the field the way Faraday did,

176
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:26,150
with lines.

177
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:27,670
The rules are simple.

178
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:31,420
A field line begins on a positive charge and ends on a

179
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:32,470
negative one.

180
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:35,980
At every point, the direction of the line is the direction

181
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,070
of the field.

182
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:40,620
And where the lines are close together the field is strong,

183
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,350
while where they spread apart it is weak.

184
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,540
Put a positive and a negative charge side by side,

185
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:49,150
and the lines arc from one to the other.

186
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,620
Put two positive charges side by side,

187
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:54,780
and the lines swerve apart, as if the two patterns were

188
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:55,910
pushing on each other.

189
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,900
Field lines never cross, because at any point the field

190
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:01,870
can only point one way.

191
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:03,830
But remember what the lines are.

192
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:07,140
They are a drawing, like the contour lines on a map of

193
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:07,980
a hill.

194
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,460
The field is not only on the lines.

195
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,510
It fills all of the space between them as well.

196
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:18,367
One arrangement matters more than any other in electrical

197
00:09:18,387 --> 00:09:19,177
engineering.

198
00:09:19,667 --> 00:09:23,167
Take two flat metal plates, facing each other,

199
00:09:23,187 --> 00:09:25,737
one charged positive and one negative.

200
00:09:26,227 --> 00:09:29,407
Between them, the field lines run straight across,

201
00:09:29,427 --> 00:09:30,857
evenly spaced.

202
00:09:31,027 --> 00:09:34,057
The field has the same strength and the same direction

203
00:09:34,147 --> 00:09:35,417
everywhere in the gap.

204
00:09:35,827 --> 00:09:39,407
We call that a uniform field, and you will meet it again

205
00:09:39,427 --> 00:09:42,297
in the very next video, inside a capacitor.

206
00:09:42,787 --> 00:09:46,447
Release an electron in that gap and it accelerates steadily

207
00:09:46,467 --> 00:09:47,977
toward the positive plate.

208
00:09:48,387 --> 00:09:50,527
It moves against the field arrows,

209
00:09:50,547 --> 00:09:52,377
because its charge is negative.

210
00:09:53,027 --> 00:09:55,537
Now put a piece of metal into a field.

211
00:09:55,667 --> 00:09:59,087
A metal is full of electrons that are free to move,

212
00:09:59,107 --> 00:09:59,897
so they do.

213
00:10:00,387 --> 00:10:04,367
They shift until their own field exactly cancels the field

214
00:10:04,387 --> 00:10:05,737
inside the metal.

215
00:10:05,907 --> 00:10:09,247
In a fraction of a second, the field inside a conductor

216
00:10:09,267 --> 00:10:13,457
is zero, and any extra charge sits on its surface.

217
00:10:13,747 --> 00:10:17,087
Faraday proved this in eighteen thirty-six in the most

218
00:10:17,107 --> 00:10:18,617
direct way possible.

219
00:10:18,787 --> 00:10:22,447
He built a wooden cube, twelve feet on each side,

220
00:10:22,467 --> 00:10:24,607
covered it in metal foil and wire,

221
00:10:24,627 --> 00:10:28,297
and sat inside it with his most sensitive instruments.

222
00:10:28,467 --> 00:10:32,047
His assistants charged the outside until sparks flew from

223
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:33,137
its surface.

224
00:10:33,267 --> 00:10:35,817
Inside, he could detect nothing at all.

225
00:10:36,307 --> 00:10:39,497
We still call such an enclosure a Faraday cage.

226
00:10:39,907 --> 00:10:43,487
It is why you are safe inside a car in a thunderstorm:

227
00:10:43,507 --> 00:10:46,247
the metal body carries the charge around you,

228
00:10:46,267 --> 00:10:48,937
and the field inside stays close to zero.

229
00:10:49,507 --> 00:10:53,007
Engineers call this shielding, and it is why sensitive

230
00:10:53,027 --> 00:10:55,617
cables are wrapped in a metal screen.

231
00:10:55,907 --> 00:10:58,017
There is one more thing to notice.

232
00:10:58,147 --> 00:11:02,487
On a conductor, charge crowds onto sharp points and edges,

233
00:11:02,507 --> 00:11:05,017
and the field there becomes intense.

234
00:11:05,187 --> 00:11:06,857
Franklin put this to use.

235
00:11:07,267 --> 00:11:10,377
Having shown in seventeen fifty-two that lightning is

236
00:11:10,467 --> 00:11:13,647
electricity, he fitted buildings with a pointed metal

237
00:11:13,667 --> 00:11:17,247
rod wired to the ground, and the lightning rod has protected

238
00:11:17,267 --> 00:11:18,537
them ever since.

239
00:11:19,027 --> 00:11:22,127
A high-voltage line suffers from the same effect:

240
00:11:22,147 --> 00:11:25,327
the faint glow and the crackle around a conductor at night

241
00:11:25,347 --> 00:11:28,697
is called corona, and it is wasted energy.

242
00:11:30,067 --> 00:11:33,247
Moving a charge against a field takes work,

243
00:11:33,267 --> 00:11:36,767
the same way that lifting a weight against gravity does.

244
00:11:36,787 --> 00:11:38,967
And that work is not lost.

245
00:11:38,987 --> 00:11:42,047
It is stored, ready to be given back.

246
00:11:42,067 --> 00:11:45,247
The work needed for each coulomb of charge is called the

247
00:11:45,267 --> 00:11:47,007
potential difference.

248
00:11:47,027 --> 00:11:49,167
You already know it by another name:

249
00:11:49,187 --> 00:11:50,447
voltage.

250
00:11:50,467 --> 00:11:53,007
The unit honours Alessandro Volta,

251
00:11:53,027 --> 00:11:56,727
who in eighteen hundred stacked discs of zinc and copper

252
00:11:56,747 --> 00:12:00,607
into the first battery, and gave the world its first steady

253
00:12:00,627 --> 00:12:02,767
source of electrical push.

254
00:12:02,787 --> 00:12:07,337
One volt means one joule of work for each coulomb moved.

255
00:12:07,387 --> 00:12:11,207
Between our two plates, voltage and field are tied together

256
00:12:11,227 --> 00:12:12,367
very simply.

257
00:12:12,387 --> 00:12:15,967
The field equals the voltage divided by the gap:

258
00:12:15,987 --> 00:12:18,407
E equals V over d.

259
00:12:18,427 --> 00:12:22,047
So the field can also be measured in volts per metre.

260
00:12:22,067 --> 00:12:24,927
Put one volt across a one-millimetre gap,

261
00:12:24,947 --> 00:12:27,807
and the field is a thousand volts per metre.

262
00:12:27,827 --> 00:12:31,207
Squeeze the same voltage into a smaller gap and the field

263
00:12:31,227 --> 00:12:32,287
grows.

264
00:12:32,307 --> 00:12:34,687
And every insulator has a limit.

265
00:12:34,707 --> 00:12:38,047
For air, it is about three million volts per metre.

266
00:12:38,067 --> 00:12:41,927
Push past that, and the air itself is torn apart and starts

267
00:12:41,947 --> 00:12:43,007
to conduct.

268
00:12:43,027 --> 00:12:44,740
That is a spark.

269
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:48,047
On a much larger scale, that is lightning.

270
00:12:48,067 --> 00:12:50,527
This is why insulation thickness,

271
00:12:50,547 --> 00:12:53,807
spacing between conductors and the smooth shape of high-voltage

272
00:12:53,827 --> 00:12:56,180
equipment are never arbitrary.

273
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:59,577
They are all ways of keeping the field below the limit.

274
00:13:00,933 --> 00:13:02,443
So let's put it together.

275
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:05,153
It began with a piece of amber,

276
00:13:05,173 --> 00:13:08,123
and it took twenty-five centuries to understand.

277
00:13:08,533 --> 00:13:10,523
Charge comes in two kinds.

278
00:13:10,613 --> 00:13:14,193
Like charges repel, opposite charges attract,

279
00:13:14,213 --> 00:13:17,153
and charge is never created or destroyed,

280
00:13:17,173 --> 00:13:18,353
only moved.

281
00:13:18,373 --> 00:13:22,123
The force between two charges grows with each charge and

282
00:13:22,173 --> 00:13:24,433
falls with the square of the distance.

283
00:13:24,453 --> 00:13:28,113
A charge fills the space around it with an electric field:

284
00:13:28,133 --> 00:13:31,153
the force that each unit of charge would feel at every

285
00:13:31,173 --> 00:13:32,033
point.

286
00:13:32,053 --> 00:13:36,033
We draw that field with lines, from positive to negative,

287
00:13:36,053 --> 00:13:37,963
crowded where it is strong.

288
00:13:38,053 --> 00:13:40,753
Inside a conductor the field is zero.

289
00:13:40,773 --> 00:13:43,113
Between two plates it is uniform,

290
00:13:43,133 --> 00:13:46,233
and equal to the voltage divided by the gap.

291
00:13:46,253 --> 00:13:49,073
Next time, we put those two plates to work,

292
00:13:49,093 --> 00:13:52,283
and store energy in the empty space between them.
